checks_run.actual:
The disclosure file is read from the tool's own directory, not from docs/delivery/** (D-138). --disclosures overrides it from the CLI.

parseDisclosures validates strictly:
- exact keys, and a format string;
- a kebab id, no duplicates;
- every string one trimmed line (no CR/LF);
- heading and link.text as [en, ar] pairs;
- link.href limited to [A-Za-z0-9_-] path segments ending .md with an optional lower-case fragment. No '.' segment is possible, so there is no traversal, URL, scheme or ')' injection.
An invalid file raises DisclosureError, which leads to exit 2 before anything is written.

Probe: all 22 hostile cases were refused with DisclosureError. They include ../, a/../../, ./, absolute, javascript:, data:, //host, ')' injection, backslash, %2e%2e, non-.md, CR, leading space, a non-string, 3-item heading, multi-line source, __proto__ (raw and parsed), array or null root, and an id with a dot. The committed input parses (1 entry). Probe exit 0.

No clock or network is read, and the output is ordered as in the file, so --check compares bytes (check 21).

Tests: 'DO-D10 disclosures: the checked-in input carries the D-278 transfer-drill rule' fails if the entry or its key phrases are removed. Other tests cover the None line, BLOCKED on 11 invalid variants and a missing file, and the committed release notes carrying every line.

The Arabic section carries the H.3 provisional lines, and check-handover raised no warning for it. Markdown in en/ar is not escaped, but it is checked-in, reviewed text, not user input.

checks_run.procedure:
In the clone at the source commit, run:
- secret-scan (payload and history);
- claims-scan;
- check-handover (default and MTH_STRICT_HANDOVER=1) with the orchestrator's protocol-audit-r7.json;
- known-limitations --check;
- release-notes --check (never --write).
Then confirm git status is unchanged. Also secret-scan my own evidence directory.

In the clone, run the full release-tool and licence test suites. Investigate every failure and skip, and re-run the affected tests with the missing environment supplied:
- T-A5 with a writable store copy;
- T-A11 on a runtime tree assembled by deploy/scripts/assemble-runtime.sh in a second disposable copy.

Confirm that no product, migration, image, scanner-rule, migration-tool, seal/verify or gate-tool code changed since round 6. If none did, the earlier rounds' source and security review of those surfaces stands for this candidate:
- the migration manifest, import and export with append-only audit;
- config bundle import;
- the OIDC loopback exception (D-258);
- the secret and asset scanners (DO-J2, DO-E6);
- the image bundle and Dockerfile (DO-E3/E4);
- seal and verify.

